diagnosis_text:
I exhaustively checked the path from PC12 (120.1.1.10) to its gateway (r12, 120.1.1.1) and upstream. On the LAN segment GG, link state and quality are clean: PC12 eth0 and r12 eth1 are UP with no input errors, drops, or carrier issues; ethtool reports 10G/full and “Link detected: yes.” Short-burst high-frequency pings from PC12 to the gateway (120.1.1.1) show 0% loss and low jitter. I then extended testing through the gateway to r11 (112.1.1.1) and r11’s OSPF interface (111.1.1.1); both runs delivered 0% loss with sub‑millisecond RTTs, indicating the issue is not present now.

On the routing plane, r12’s EIGRP peering to r11 and r11’s OSPF adjacency are established and stable. FRR logs on both routers show neighbors coming up at boot and no subsequent flaps. Kernel and FRR routing tables on r12 and r11 are consistent, and there is no evidence of churn that would cause periodic blackholes. Traffic control (fq_codel) counters on r12 and PC12 show no queue drops.

Mechanism-wise, the symptoms described (brief pauses a few times an hour) would likely show as packet loss or interface errors if caused by L2/L3 instability; none are observable now. Localization steps pinpoint the key segments (PC12↔r12 eth1, r12 eth0↔r11) as healthy.

Root cause: Unable to reproduce any network-layer fault; current evidence shows normal operation.

Corrective fix: No network changes applied. To capture an intermittent condition if it recurs, please run a continuous mtr or ping from PC12 to 120.1.1.1 and 112.1.1.1 for at least an hour and note timestamps. I can then correlate with FRR logs and interface stats to isolate any transient events. 
=== END ===